WEBVTT

00:00:00.453 --> 00:00:04.472
CUBE expands the idea further by producing every grouping combination.

00:00:04.472 --> 00:00:08.512
With category and product, that includes category-and-product detail, category totals,

00:00:08.512 --> 00:00:13.232
product totals across categories, and the grand total.

00:00:13.232 --> 00:00:17.602
Run the example and compare the number and type of rows with the ROLLUP result.

00:00:17.602 --> 00:00:20.727
The key difference is that ROLLUP follows a hierarchy,

00:00:20.727 --> 00:00:24.362
while CUBE creates totals for every dimension combination.

00:00:24.362 --> 00:00:31.837
CUBE can be useful for exploratory summary tables, but the number of combinations grows quickly when you add

00:00:31.837 --> 00:00:32.162
more dimensions.

00:00:32.162 --> 00:00:37.482
Before using CUBE in a larger dataset, think about how many grouping combinations you are requesting and

00:00:37.482 --> 00:00:40.797
whether each one supports a real analytical need.

00:00:40.797 --> 00:00:45.067
Advanced grouping produces several aggregation levels in one result.

00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:51.807
The challenge is interpretation: NULL may mean a subtotal placeholder rather than missing source data.

00:00:51.807 --> 00:00:57.577
Use GROUPING metadata or explicit labels so downstream users can distinguish detail groups, subtotals,

00:00:57.577 --> 00:00:59.120
and grand totals.
